# RT100 hardware safety (non-negotiable)

The screen has no JTAG and its bootloader has no fallback: a firmware that
crashes at boot bricks it permanently. The keyboard and screen are the user's
only units.

## Never, without the user explicitly saying yes in this conversation
- Run `oled_flash.py` (any arguments other than `--dry-run`) or anything that flashes.
- Send vendor command `0x30` (enter bootloader), `0xB1`, or raw `rt100.send(...)` packets.
- Send any vendor command not produced by `rt100screen.py` / `screenproto.py`.
- Use `rt100screen.py --dongle` (passthrough over 2.4 GHz is untested).
- Change keyboard settings, keymaps or keyboard firmware.

## Allowed on the device without asking
- `rt100screen.py run|text|image|settime|stock` (add `--dry-run` first when you changed encoding code).
- Read-only queries via `rt100.query`: `0x80` (keyboard version), `0x8F` (dev id), `0xAD` (screen version).

## Rules for talking to the device
- Only one sender at a time. Before sending, check nothing else is running:
  `pgrep -af rt100screen` — two senders interleave passthrough sessions and corrupt them.
- A passthrough session (`0x31 <count>` + exactly `count` packets) must always be completed.
  `Link._session` pads with no-op packets in `finally`; never bypass `Link`.
- Never `kill -9` a sender mid-session. If the keyboard stops responding to the
  tools, tell the user to unplug and replug the keyboard; do not improvise commands.
- If the screen shows something unexpected (dark, garbled, rotated), stop sending,
  run `rt100screen.py stock`, and report to the user. Do not "try things".

## Files you must not modify
- `re/fw/**` (original firmware dumps), `re/decomp/**`, `re/patch/**` (flashed I2C fix).
- `re/custom/out/firmwareOledFile.bin` is what is flashed now; only `re/custom/build.py` writes it.

When in doubt: stop and ask the user. A slower answer is fine; a bricked screen is not.
